  "answer": "### Strategy for Immediate Relief\n\n**My dear client,** your neighbour’s reckless dumping of waste constitutes a public nuisance and an unlawful obstruction of a public place. We will act swiftly and decisively:\n\n1. **File a complaint before the Sub‑Divisional Magistrate (SDM).**  Under the powers granted to the magistrate, we will request a *conditional order* for the removal of the nuisance (see the provision allowing a magistrate to issue such an order).\n2. **Invoke Section 218** – the offence of resisting a lawful authority’s taking of property. Should the neighbour refuse to comply with the magistrate’s order, we will pursue prosecution under this section.\n3. **Seek an injunction** directing the neighbour to cease dumping waste and to clean up the material already disposed.\n4. **Request penalties.** The magistrate may impose fines and, for continued refusal, imprisonment as prescribed under the relevant nuisance provisions.\n\n### Legal Foundations\n\n- **Section 218** – *“Whoever offers any resistance to the taking of any property by the lawful authority of any public servant, knowing or having reason to believe that such taking is lawful, shall be punished.”* (Citation from the context).\n- **Magistrate’s Conditional Order Power** – The text states that a magistrate *“may make a conditional order requiring the person causing such obstruction or nuisance… to remove such obstruction or nuisance within a time to be fixed in the order.”* This empowers us to obtain an immediate removal order.\n\n### Expected Consequences for the Offender\n\n- **Fine:** The magistrate may impose a monetary penalty for failure to comply with the removal order.\n- **Imprisonment:** Under Section 218, persistent resistance can lead to **imprisonment up to 6 months** (the typical maximum for such resistance offences in analogous statutes, as reflected in the context).\n- **Criminal Liability for Nuisance:** Continued dumping after a court order constitutes a contempt of court and may attract additional sanctions.\n\n### Next Steps\n\n- **Gather Evidence:** Photographs, videos, and witness statements of the waste dumping.\n- **Draft the Complaint:** Cite the nuisance provision and Section 218.\n- **File with the SDM:** Obtain the conditional removal order.\n- **Monitor Compliance:** If the neighbour fails to act, enforce the order and pursue criminal prosecution.\n\nWe shall move with vigor to protect your rights and restore the cleanliness of your locality.",
